00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:39.530
It's Google, so
data is encrypted,

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:43.290
data is durable,
highly available.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:46.120
The key part of
BigQuery is that it's

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:49.340
serverless and
fully managed, which

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:53.630
means all you care about
is what data you have,

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:55.650
how you want to
analyze it, and let

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:58.520
us take care of how many
nodes you need in the back.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:01.430
So the concept of node does not
exist, which means your life

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:02.910
is much, much easier.

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:07.970
And finally, you can actually
use it for real time analytics

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:10.009
as well.

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:11.500
So what does it mean?

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:16.420
A lot of you who have used
BigQuery can attest to the fact

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:20.410
that having it being
serverless has really

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:25.570
changed how data warehousing is
done, as your life has become

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:29.530
much, much easier by
focusing just on the analysis

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:32.770
and not worry about
how it will scale when

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:35.260
you go to petabytes of data.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:38.740
With BigQuery ML, we
are bringing the same

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:40.850
to machine learning.

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:43.800
People say AI is
changing the world,

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:47.760
but in reality,
even though you can

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.860
think of use cases which
can become predictive,

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:55.630
it's very, very hard to
actually use machine learning.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:59.400
So that's what we are
doing with BigQuery ML.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:03.930
And to illustrate it, let's
look at how machine learning may

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:07.950
look like in a couple of forms.

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:11.460
Let's say you have a few
terabytes of data in your data

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:14.490
warehouse-- it could be
BigQuery or a different one.

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:16.570
How do you do machine learning?

00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:20.070
The first step in almost
every instance we have seen

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:22.530
is to export the data out.

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:24.210
So you go discover
the data you want

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:27.630
to use for training your
model, you export it.

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:30.960
If you're lucky to have
a data science team,

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:36.000
they will format it to your
favorite Python framework,

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.070
and come back to you
in a few weeks saying,

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:39.930
we need more data.

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:43.410
And you just take months
to build that model.

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:46.310
If you don't have a data
science team, you just

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:50.250
take a very, very small
sample, you move it to Excel,

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:52.410
and you just run
basic regression.

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:54.060
And I have talked
to customers who

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:57.930
say even the Excel-based
approach takes months

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:00.220
to build a modeling of data.

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:04.565
So the two underlying
problems for ML

00:04:04.565 --> 00:04:08.550
are, one, doing
even the smallest ML

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:13.320
project needs data
scientists, and there are just

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:16.570
too few of them.

00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:19.065
It requires so much
understanding of math

00:04:19.065 --> 00:04:22.430
or so much understanding
of hyperparameter tuning

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:24.490
that that becomes a bottleneck.

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:29.520
The second is it
takes multiple tools

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:32.520
and it takes moving
data out to even start

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:35.250
a machine learning project.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:40.610
And all these are just
productivity hassles

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:46.370
that prevent you from trying
out the cool ML stuff you

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:48.300
have for your analysis.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:51.290
So that's what
BigQuery ML enables.

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:55.160
We are introducing a way to
do machine learning using

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:58.730
SQL string within BigQuery.

00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:01.710
Right where you write
your select statement,

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:04.240
you just start doing
machine learning

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:07.620
on the data you already have.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:10.070
So what do you get from this?

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:13.790
You can build your own
machine learning models

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:15.560
in standard SQL.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:19.190
You can predict in
standard SQL, as well.

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:22.310
The models stay within BigQuery.

00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:25.430
You can train it
on whatever data

00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:28.160
you want to train it on
within BigQuery or data

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:31.710
that BigQuery federates
from other sources.

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:36.840
And finally, you only have to
worry about the data and SQL.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:40.820
We take of the underlying
hyperparameter tuning.

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:44.330
And we take of feature
transformation standardization

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:46.399
and other things
that usually are part

00:05:46.399 --> 00:05:47.690
of a machine learning workflow.

00:05:50.390 --> 00:05:54.240
To give you a more
practical example,

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:58.590
let's say you have marketing
data coming into BigQuery.

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:01.100
So you get data from
your website visitors

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:04.430
from Google Analytics,
you have first party data

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:09.740
from your customer relationship
management platform.

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:11.720
You move it to
BigQuery because that's

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:14.940
where it's so easy to
[INAUDIBLE] and analyze it.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:18.150
Now, rather than just
looking at historical trends,

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:21.570
if you wanted to start making
predictions for your marketing

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:26.570
counterparts, you would write
a model using BigQuery ML.

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:29.170
You would take
those predictions,

00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:33.520
report it out to your
favorite BI platform,

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:36.420
and you would productionalize
it by connecting it

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:38.970
with your marketing
automation systems

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:41.320
to take action based
on these predictions.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:46.410
And in all of this, since you do
it in BigQuery, it is all SQL.

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:49.950
It's very, very easy to
set this workflow up.

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:54.270
And when you want to retrain
this model with new data,

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:56.940
you just use Airflow
or Cloud Composer.

00:06:56.940 --> 00:06:59.700
So you can actually get to
a fully production model--

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:03.660
and we'll have Naveed talk
about it later in this session--

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:05.970
staying within the BigQuery.

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:08.800
I'll give a few
more examples to get

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:10.740
you thinking about
what kind of use cases

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:13.440
it can empower you for.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:17.790
So these are some of our
