00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:23.130
I will show you a quick
demo of how this works.

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:26.220
To set it up, let's
say you are a marketer

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:29.410
at a retail e-commerce company.

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:32.400
And one of the very
common problems

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:34.680
is that if you have a
million visitors a month

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:37.620
to your website, how do
you plan your marketing

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:40.560
campaigns to them?

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:44.120
We actually have data from
the Google merchandise store

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:47.810
that shows only 0.7%
of website visitors

00:09:47.810 --> 00:09:50.900
actually come back
and make a purchase.

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:55.430
With that low return on
investment from your marketing,

00:09:55.430 --> 00:09:57.830
you're not going to
target all of them.

00:09:57.830 --> 00:10:04.080
So in this example I'll show
you how, using machine learning,

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:07.580
you can do a much, much better
job at planning your marketing

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:08.690
campaigns.

00:10:08.690 --> 00:10:11.780
And if you can please
switch to the demo screen.

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:25.740
So this is the new BigQuery
UI that we launched recently.

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:28.900
And I'm sure a lot of you
are familiar with their data.

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:33.740
So what it shows is we
have this public dataset

00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:39.050
from Google Analytics from
the Google merchandise store.

00:10:39.050 --> 00:10:44.380
We are selecting a few
free columns from this

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:46.120
to use as features.

00:10:46.120 --> 00:10:50.110
And if you think about marketing
data, some of these features

00:10:50.110 --> 00:10:53.760
are very, very obvious
that you believe

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:59.280
are going to have predictive
influence on if a person will

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:01.640
come back and purchase or not.

00:11:01.640 --> 00:11:05.040
And you just select these, like
how many page views happened

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:06.120
in a session?

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:07.740
Did the person buy?

00:11:07.740 --> 00:11:10.860
Where did the person come from?

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:12.420
Was it from a desktop?

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:14.220
Was it from a cell phone?

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:15.600
Or what country was it?

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:17.520
So you select these.

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:21.220
You do not select the person's
ID because that's not useful.

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:27.385
And all you do is you say,
I want to create a model.

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:36.380
And you give it a name.

00:11:36.380 --> 00:11:40.340
The only options you need to
give is what type of a model

00:11:40.340 --> 00:11:41.000
it is.

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:44.240
We support linear and
logistic regression

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:46.310
for forecasting
or classification

00:11:46.310 --> 00:11:47.940
based on your use case.

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:53.900
So in this case, you are going
to predict a label that says,

00:11:53.900 --> 00:11:58.930
will this visitor
come back and buy?

00:11:58.930 --> 00:12:00.460
And just telling
us what the label

00:12:00.460 --> 00:12:05.620
is on top of your
regular SQL query--

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:08.600
in this example, we took the
first nine months of data

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:09.660
to train this model.

00:12:12.260 --> 00:12:15.560
You just simply let it go.

00:12:15.560 --> 00:12:18.920
And this will take a
few minutes to finish.

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:25.640
It's working on a very
large set of complex data.

00:12:25.640 --> 00:12:28.790
But what you'll get
at the end of it--

00:12:28.790 --> 00:12:32.690
once you have this model,
you use it for predictions,

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:36.980
you can build a report, which
is what any marketer does.

00:12:36.980 --> 00:12:39.740
In this case, it's
on Data Studio

00:12:39.740 --> 00:12:46.810
that shows that if you
were to just target

00:12:46.810 --> 00:12:54.870
the top 6% of the scored
leads, you actually

00:12:54.870 --> 00:12:57.930
get nine times the people
who come back and purchase.

00:12:57.930 --> 00:13:01.570
And this was on the last
three months of test data.

00:13:01.570 --> 00:13:06.590
And furthermore, you capture
50% of the potential buyers.

00:13:06.590 --> 00:13:11.100
So both the precision and
the recall of this model

00:13:11.100 --> 00:13:13.060
is really high.

00:13:13.060 --> 00:13:17.430
And if you look back at the
create model statement we had,

00:13:17.430 --> 00:13:21.720
you actually did not have to
give any hyperparameter tuning

00:13:21.720 --> 00:13:22.360
options.

00:13:22.360 --> 00:13:25.110
You did not have to do
anything, except just select

00:13:25.110 --> 00:13:26.610
the right features.

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:28.500
And if you selected
more that don't matter,

00:13:28.500 --> 00:13:32.370
those will be ignored
with a low rate anyways.

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:38.540
So in this example, with a
couple of lines of SQL code,

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:41.960
you actually can manage
to get nine times

00:13:41.960 --> 00:13:44.480
the ROI on your
marketing campaigns,

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:49.598
still reaching 50%
of the population.

00:13:49.598 --> 00:14:10.190
And if you were to think about
the typical machine learning

00:14:10.190 --> 00:14:14.260
metrics, this model
looks really good.

00:14:14.260 --> 00:14:19.790
The ROC curve here is
actually the best you can get.

00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:24.320
It's very hard to get
a better ROC curve.

00:14:24.320 --> 00:14:28.260
And similarly, the precision and
recall curves are really good.

00:14:28.260 --> 00:14:33.840
So through BigQuery ML, you
got a model you could predict.

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:35.790
And those of you who
actually want to dive

00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:38.630
into the performance metrics,
it's really easy to do that,

00:14:38.630 --> 00:14:39.130
as well.

00:14:41.950 --> 00:14:45.420
Let's see if it's still running.

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:49.740
If we can go back to the
presentation, please.

00:14:49.740 --> 00:14:50.240
Thank you.

00:14:53.520 --> 00:14:55.670
So what happened
behind the scenes?

00:14:55.670 --> 00:14:58.400
You go to Create Model.

00:14:58.400 --> 00:15:03.140
You did not have to think about
the infrastructure at all.

00:15:03.140 --> 00:15:09.210
It created a bunch of queries
on BigQuery, highly parallelized

00:15:09.210 --> 00:15:12.230
tech, and give you the answer.

00:15:12.230 --> 00:15:15.410
So determining the
infrastructure, thinking

00:15:15.410 --> 00:15:20.090
about how many compute hours I
would need, do I have two VMs,

00:15:20.090 --> 00:15:23.400
do I have six VMs, all
that is distraction.

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:26.660
With BigQuery, you build
a model without having

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:29.750
to think anything about it.

00:15:29.750 --> 00:15:32.050
Second, there's a
hyperparameter called

00:15:32.050 --> 00:15:35.740
learning rate, which
is how fast you

00:15:35.740 --> 00:15:38.130
want to change your parameters.

00:15:38.130 --> 00:15:40.900
We autotune that one.

00:15:40.900 --> 00:15:45.030
You never want to create your
model on all of training data.

00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:48.110
So to prevent overfitting,
we auto-split data

00:15:48.110 --> 00:15:49.420
into training insight.

00:15:49.420 --> 00:15:52.360
You did not have to do that
in that example either.

00:15:52.360 --> 00:15:55.510
When you think about
numeric features,

00:15:55.510 --> 00:15:58.740
we automatically
standardize those.

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:02.390
Again, it's a very basic
task-- has to be done.

00:16:02.390 --> 00:16:03.910
Why leave it to the user?

00:16:03.910 --> 00:16:05.160
We just do it.

00:16:05.160 --> 00:16:06.580
And finally, for
string features,

00:16:06.580 --> 00:16:09.470
we one-hot encode them.

00:16:09.470 --> 00:16:13.250
So anything that needs to
be a category, we see it

00:16:13.250 --> 00:16:15.170
and we one-hot encode it.

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:16.910
And this is just a start.

00:16:16.910 --> 00:16:22.040
As you think about automation of
all these basic transformations

00:16:22.040 --> 00:16:26.210
and feature engineering, we'll
just keep on adding to it.

00:16:26.210 --> 00:16:29.990
And this should suffice
a lot of the use cases.

00:16:29.990 --> 00:16:34.770
For the advanced user
who may want to do more,

00:16:34.770 --> 00:16:36.710
we actually do give
the [? nodes. ?]

00:16:36.710 --> 00:16:41.570
So if you want to use, say, L1
regularization so you get rates

00:16:41.570 --> 00:16:45.860
close to zero or one
for different features,

00:16:45.860 --> 00:16:47.890
you have that option.

00:16:47.890 --> 00:16:53.570
You say, a random training
test data split does not work.

00:16:53.570 --> 00:16:55.550
I want to do sequential.

00:16:55.550 --> 00:16:58.250
Well, I want to flag
and split them myself.

00:16:58.250 --> 00:16:59.685
You can do that, as well.

00:16:59.685 --> 00:17:01.310
You want to set your
own learning rate,

00:17:01.310 --> 00:17:02.730
you can do that, as well.

00:17:02.730 --> 00:17:07.950
So the philosophy is you get
a great model out of the box.

00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:10.280
And if you want to
further fine-tune it,

00:17:10.280 --> 00:17:12.210
we have the options for you.

00:17:15.740 --> 00:17:18.050
Thinking about what we support--

00:17:18.050 --> 00:17:21.619
so you can use StandardSQL,
you can use UDFs

00:17:21.619 --> 00:17:23.270
within the queries.

00:17:23.270 --> 00:17:25.790
If you have a
forecasting use case--

00:17:25.790 --> 00:17:28.890
let's say you want to
forecast the sales of a SKU,

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:33.020
you want to forecast the number
of users on your website,

00:17:33.020 --> 00:17:36.200
you can do it using
linear regression.

00:17:36.200 --> 00:17:39.710
If you want to classify, say,
will a user come back and make

00:17:39.710 --> 00:17:42.770
a purchase is in this example,
or if you wanted to say,

00:17:42.770 --> 00:17:47.030
will a user subscribe, will a
user churn, all those things

00:17:47.030 --> 00:17:50.310
you can do using
logistic regression.

00:17:50.310 --> 00:17:55.250
For evaluation of models, we
have table valued functions

00:17:55.250 --> 00:17:58.610
that you can use to get all
standard metrics that you

00:17:58.610 --> 00:18:01.360
may be used to if you already
know some machine learning.

00:18:01.360 --> 00:18:03.110
And for logistic
regression, you can

00:18:03.110 --> 00:18:08.180
get the ROC curve and precision
recall numbers, as well.

00:18:08.180 --> 00:18:11.210
If you want to look
at the model weights

00:18:11.210 --> 00:18:14.930
to explain to someone which
features mattered more

00:18:14.930 --> 00:18:18.380
and which mattered less, we
have an ml.weights [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:18.380 --> 00:18:19.790
for that one.

00:18:19.790 --> 00:18:24.480
And finally, to do your
feature engineering,

00:18:24.480 --> 00:18:27.680
there's a function
called ml.featureinfo.

00:18:27.680 --> 00:18:31.010
And you can use that function
to see standard stats,

00:18:31.010 --> 00:18:35.626
like min/max, standard dev,
on each of these features that

00:18:35.626 --> 00:18:36.500
went into your model.

00:18:44.130 --> 00:18:50.140
As we announced it, we realized
that BigQuery UI is not

00:18:50.140 --> 00:18:54.110
the only place that
our users use us from.

00:18:54.110 --> 00:19:00.340
So we have integrations
with Data Studio, Looker,

00:19:00.340 --> 00:19:02.260
and Tableau.

00:19:02.260 --> 00:19:06.290
And if you go through
your documentation,

00:19:06.290 --> 00:19:07.860
you can find out more.

00:19:07.860 --> 00:19:11.120
But in this example,
on the left,

00:19:11.120 --> 00:19:17.360
Tableau has built a UI-based
housing price prediction tool,

00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:18.750
which is a very hot topic.

00:19:18.750 --> 00:19:22.250
But in this case, using a
model built in BigQuery ML,

00:19:22.250 --> 00:19:25.430
you can get predictions
out of the box.

00:19:25.430 --> 00:19:27.640
On the right, you see the
Data Studio performance

00:19:27.640 --> 00:19:28.950
report I just showed.

00:19:28.950 --> 00:19:34.130
Add at the bottom you see the
performance report on Looker.

00:19:34.130 --> 00:19:37.730
And with Looker, we actually
have the deepest integration.

00:19:37.730 --> 00:19:40.190
So one, they have
blocks so you can

00:19:40.190 --> 00:19:42.500
explore the data in BigQuery.

00:19:42.500 --> 00:19:46.070
Second, using LookML, which
is their markup language,

00:19:46.070 --> 00:19:50.090
you can actually create
models staying within Looker.

00:19:50.090 --> 00:19:55.451
And finally, you can
operationalize your ML workflow

00:19:55.451 --> 00:19:56.450
through Looker, as well.

00:20:01.660 --> 00:20:05.380
If you take a step back
and think where it fits--

00:20:05.380 --> 00:20:09.040
because everyone
talks a lot about ML,

00:20:09.040 --> 00:20:11.230
Google announces
things in TensorFlow,

00:20:11.230 --> 00:20:13.730
things about AutoML--

00:20:13.730 --> 00:20:15.790
where does BigQuery ML fit?

00:20:15.790 --> 00:20:20.680
We see this as a
centerpiece in making ML

00:20:20.680 --> 00:20:23.820
accessible for all audiences.

00:20:23.820 --> 00:20:28.080
TensorFlow Cloud ML Engine
is for the data scientist

00:20:28.080 --> 00:20:32.990
to build the state of the art
neural network-based model

00:20:32.990 --> 00:20:37.820
that's required for a very
high performance application.

00:20:37.820 --> 00:20:44.650
AutoML leverages the Google
IP on standard problem sets,

00:20:44.650 --> 00:20:47.680
like object detection
in images, object

00:20:47.680 --> 00:20:52.150
detection in videos, semantic
understanding on text,

00:20:52.150 --> 00:20:55.420
and gives a
developer the ability

00:20:55.420 --> 00:20:58.840
to use that as an API
to build those models.

00:20:58.840 --> 00:21:00.550
But what about
all these business

00:21:00.550 --> 00:21:05.970
use cases for which
speed is more important

00:21:05.970 --> 00:21:08.410
and for which it's more
important to have someone who

00:21:08.410 --> 00:21:12.130
understands their data to
build a model using the tools

00:21:12.130 --> 00:21:13.000
you know?

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:15.430
That's what BigQuery ML is for.

00:21:15.430 --> 00:21:18.310
It's for our BigQuery
data analysts

00:21:18.310 --> 00:21:22.930
who work with data day
in and day out, love SQL.

00:21:22.930 --> 00:21:25.630
But it's probably
too much to ask

00:21:25.630 --> 00:21:29.440
to have them go
learn TensorFlow,

00:21:29.440 --> 00:21:33.550
go set up five different
tools, move it out of BigQuery

00:21:33.550 --> 00:21:35.620
just to be able
to build a model.

00:21:35.620 --> 00:21:39.610
So that's where it fits
within our portfolio.

00:21:39.610 --> 00:21:45.230
We have AutoML for developers,
BigQuery ML for analysts,

00:21:45.230 --> 00:21:49.000
and TensorFlow CMLE
for data scientists.

00:21:52.260 --> 00:21:56.350
And if you think about the use
cases in different industries,

00:21:56.350 --> 00:21:59.220
any business use case you see--

00:21:59.220 --> 00:22:01.670
from prediction
of lifetime value

00:22:01.670 --> 00:22:03.800
to if you are in
a gaming company,

00:22:03.800 --> 00:22:05.810
what action will
this player take?

00:22:05.810 --> 00:22:07.960
Will this player come
back the next day?

00:22:07.960 --> 00:22:12.110
If you think about IoT,
downtime prediction.

00:22:12.110 --> 00:22:15.200
So things which
require a lot of data

00:22:15.200 --> 00:22:19.450
that is or can be in
BigQuery, which is structured,

00:22:19.450 --> 00:22:23.980
you can use BigQuery ML to start
approaching those use cases.

00:22:23.980 --> 00:22:27.050
It's really easy to
get started with.

00:22:27.050 --> 00:22:30.400
And once you get going, then
you can improve your models

00:22:30.400 --> 00:22:33.530
and put them into production.

00:22:33.530 --> 00:22:38.090
With that, I'm going to
call Naveed from Hearst

00:22:38.090 --> 00:22:41.930
to talk about their
experience with BigQuery ML

00:22:41.930 --> 00:22:45.450
and how they have actually
implemented it in production.

00:22:45.450 --> 00:22:46.215
Naveed.

00:22:46.215 --> 00:22:49.680
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:53.787 --> 00:22:54.620
NAVEED AHMAD: Hello.

00:22:54.620 --> 00:22:55.850
My name is Naveed Ahmad.

00:22:55.850 --> 00:22:57.950
I'm a Senior Director
for Data Engineering

00:22:57.950 --> 00:22:59.990
and Machine Learning
at Hearst Newspapers.

00:23:02.980 --> 00:23:05.830
So you might not have
heard about Hearst.

00:23:05.830 --> 00:23:09.130
Hearst is a very
large media company.

00:23:09.130 --> 00:23:11.110
You might have heard
about names such as,

00:23:11.110 --> 00:23:14.470
"San Francisco
Chronicle" or SFGate.

00:23:14.470 --> 00:23:18.970
Or magazines like "Esquire"
or "Cosmopolitan".

00:23:18.970 --> 00:23:21.490
And there's many,
many more companies

00:23:21.490 --> 00:23:25.360
under the Hearst umbrella.

00:23:25.360 --> 00:23:28.420
You can look it up on the
Hearst website or the Hearst

00:23:28.420 --> 00:23:29.410
newspapers.

00:23:29.410 --> 00:23:32.560
I'm here talking about Hearst
newspapers and its application

00:23:32.560 --> 00:23:36.160
for BigQuery ML.

00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:39.410
Hearst employs about 4,000
employees across the nation

00:23:39.410 --> 00:23:43.660
and it focuses on
local newspapers.

00:23:43.660 --> 00:23:47.620
It has about 40-plus websites
and it's continuously growing

00:23:47.620 --> 00:23:49.870
by acquiring more websites.

00:23:49.870 --> 00:23:53.500
And this is a picture of the
Hearst building in New York,

00:23:53.500 --> 00:23:55.330
very close to Central Park.

00:23:57.880 --> 00:24:01.360
So why BigQuery ML
for Hearst newspapers?

00:24:01.360 --> 00:24:04.930
We are already a big
shop, a BigQuery.

00:24:04.930 --> 00:24:08.410
We have a whole bunch of
datasets already sitting

00:24:08.410 --> 00:24:12.460
in BigQuery, such as Google
Analytics, our subscription

00:24:12.460 --> 00:24:16.930
data, newsletter
usage, demographics.

00:24:16.930 --> 00:24:20.250
So since this data was
already there in BigQuery,

00:24:20.250 --> 00:24:22.330
it just made sense to--

00:24:22.330 --> 00:24:26.230
when I heard about this
feature a few months ago--

00:24:26.230 --> 00:24:30.140
let's give BigQuery ML a shot.

00:24:30.140 --> 00:24:35.590
So another thing is, anybody who
is familiar with the SQL syntax

00:24:35.590 --> 00:24:40.420
can really get onboard
doing data science.

00:24:40.420 --> 00:24:42.610
You need to learn
basic concepts,

00:24:42.610 --> 00:24:45.940
like precision recall
or ROC, which somebody

00:24:45.940 --> 00:24:48.050
can pick up very quickly.

00:24:48.050 --> 00:24:52.120
But you don't have to
be familiar with Python

00:24:52.120 --> 00:24:56.370
or something like sci-kit
learn or TensorFlow

00:24:56.370 --> 00:24:59.350
to be able to use this.

00:24:59.350 --> 00:25:01.120
Another benefit
is that you don't

00:25:01.120 --> 00:25:04.450
have to ETL out data,
do machine learning,

00:25:04.450 --> 00:25:08.860
do all that massaging
in a script like Python

00:25:08.860 --> 00:25:10.870
and ingest it back, and
put it into reports.

00:25:10.870 --> 00:25:15.310
That whole cycle just
gets cut down dramatically

00:25:15.310 --> 00:25:18.910
because you do
everything in BigQuery.

00:25:18.910 --> 00:25:22.215
And some of the other
goodies with BQML

00:25:22.215 --> 00:25:25.720
is it does normalization,
one-hot encoding,

00:25:25.720 --> 00:25:28.130
as Abhishek already mentioned.

00:25:28.130 --> 00:25:31.030
And then you can also,
after training the model,

00:25:31.030 --> 00:25:34.900
you can get the training,
the machine learning matrix

00:25:34.900 --> 00:25:36.610
using SQL syntax, as well.

00:25:40.060 --> 00:25:44.820
So the use case that we
developed is churn prediction.

00:25:44.820 --> 00:25:49.240
Churn prediction is to
predict given your existing

00:25:49.240 --> 00:25:50.880
active subscriber,
how likely are

00:25:50.880 --> 00:25:54.340
they to cancel their
subscription in the future?

00:25:54.340 --> 00:25:56.910
And this is a very relevant
use case for media,

00:25:56.910 --> 00:26:00.295
because you probably have
heard in news that people

00:26:00.295 --> 00:26:01.170
have lots of choices.

00:26:03.990 --> 00:26:06.660
People tend to go
from print to digital.

00:26:06.660 --> 00:26:10.950
So if we could save some
of our subscribers--

00:26:10.950 --> 00:26:14.250
I looked up some
proverb for this.

00:26:14.250 --> 00:26:16.257
Money saved is money made.

00:26:16.257 --> 00:26:18.090
Then I thought, maybe
there's other proverbs

00:26:18.090 --> 00:26:20.730
that apply to this scenario.

00:26:20.730 --> 00:26:23.850
For example, prevention
is better than cure.

00:26:23.850 --> 00:26:27.270
And I have another one that
I'll use as an Easter egg

00:26:27.270 --> 00:26:29.900
for my talk tomorrow.

00:26:29.900 --> 00:26:33.300
I'm giving a talk
about machine learning

00:26:33.300 --> 00:26:35.370
in context of publication--

00:26:35.370 --> 00:26:38.905
talks about features
about BQML, AutoML,

00:26:38.905 --> 00:26:43.650
how to apply natural language
processing and TensorFlow

00:26:43.650 --> 00:26:45.670
in the space of media.

00:26:45.670 --> 00:26:49.110
So if you find that
relevant, please attend that.

00:26:49.110 --> 00:26:52.530
And this gives insights
into future cancellation

00:26:52.530 --> 00:26:54.030
of subscriptions.

00:26:54.030 --> 00:26:56.880
And this is a binary
classification problem.

00:26:56.880 --> 00:26:58.830
People who cancelled
and who didn't.

00:26:58.830 --> 00:27:03.120
So that fits very nicely
into the logistic regression

00:27:03.120 --> 00:27:06.480
facilities offered by BQML.

00:27:06.480 --> 00:27:11.970
And we took a year's worth of
data for our subscriptions,

00:27:11.970 --> 00:27:15.480
newsletter, demographics,
web browsing behavior

00:27:15.480 --> 00:27:20.830
of a customer, and customer
service data setting in BQML.

00:27:25.370 --> 00:27:28.430
So when I put this diagram--

00:27:28.430 --> 00:27:30.980
the architecture is
really two nodes.

00:27:30.980 --> 00:27:33.320
I just tried to fill
this diagram out

00:27:33.320 --> 00:27:35.660
with all these
different steps, really.

00:27:38.470 --> 00:27:39.980
It's BigQuery and Looker.

00:27:39.980 --> 00:27:43.160
So everything you
do is in BigQuery.

00:27:43.160 --> 00:27:47.060
And our procedure really is,
we're ETLing our standard ETL

00:27:47.060 --> 00:27:51.020
process into BigQuery.

00:27:51.020 --> 00:27:53.690
And then we do
some preprocessing.

00:27:53.690 --> 00:27:59.690
We preprocess some of the more
complicated SQL, pre-create

00:27:59.690 --> 00:28:02.480
those tables, especially
with Google Analytics.

00:28:02.480 --> 00:28:05.010
The linkage of subscription
data to Google Analytics,

00:28:05.010 --> 00:28:06.800
we want to compute that before.

00:28:06.800 --> 00:28:09.260
And then once we
have all our tables,

00:28:09.260 --> 00:28:14.240
it's really a matter of running
this Create Models SQL, which

00:28:14.240 --> 00:28:17.540
basically has one big table
with a whole bunch of features

00:28:17.540 --> 00:28:20.410
from all these different
datasets and a label column

00:28:20.410 --> 00:28:24.920
which says, is this person
going to cancel or not?

00:28:24.920 --> 00:28:27.410
And we're using
last year of data,

00:28:27.410 --> 00:28:30.410
splitting it to two
halves-- six months of data

00:28:30.410 --> 00:28:31.980
where we're extracting feature.

00:28:31.980 --> 00:28:35.030
And from that
six-month point onward,

00:28:35.030 --> 00:28:39.530
we know which subscribers
cancel and who didn't.

00:28:39.530 --> 00:28:44.810
And then you run the SQL, go
for a cup of tea or coffee,

00:28:44.810 --> 00:28:48.590
and then come back and your
model is ready for usage.

00:28:48.590 --> 00:28:50.930
And then we take the
existing subscriber

00:28:50.930 --> 00:28:56.270
with the same feature set and
run it through the predict SQL.

00:28:56.270 --> 00:28:59.000
And then it produces
a probability score

00:28:59.000 --> 00:29:02.630
for each subscriber, how
likely it is to cancel.

00:29:02.630 --> 00:29:06.350
And then on top of it, we
built Looker dashboards,

00:29:06.350 --> 00:29:11.450
which display our
end data, how likely

00:29:11.450 --> 00:29:13.190
a person is to cancel or not.

00:29:13.190 --> 00:29:15.240
And also, the machine
learning matrices--

00:29:15.240 --> 00:29:18.400
we can see how good or bad
this machine learning model

00:29:18.400 --> 00:29:24.100
did, like show the
weights that it learned,

00:29:24.100 --> 00:29:27.840
just to get more idea
of what's happening.

00:29:27.840 --> 00:29:32.450
So this is a snapshot
of our dashboard.

00:29:32.450 --> 00:29:37.130
It's probably hard to see,
but over here this look

00:29:37.130 --> 00:29:39.570
shows you for each
subscription, I

00:29:39.570 --> 00:29:42.310
have erased out their
subscription IDs.

00:29:42.310 --> 00:29:44.990
Like, what is the top
people who are going

00:29:44.990 --> 00:29:47.230
to cancel their subscription?

00:29:47.230 --> 00:29:52.670
So this is something that a
marketer or a person dealing

00:29:52.670 --> 00:29:57.050
with subscription retention
can look at and take action on.

00:29:57.050 --> 00:29:59.910
Right beside there, we can
see a look at the score.

00:29:59.910 --> 00:30:02.540
This is the area
under the curve, which

00:30:02.540 --> 00:30:05.210
essentially is, in
this graph, it shows

00:30:05.210 --> 00:30:07.610
the area under this curve.

00:30:07.610 --> 00:30:14.150
And all of these matrices
you can get from the ROC SQL

00:30:14.150 --> 00:30:16.130
support in BQML.

00:30:16.130 --> 00:30:18.480
And this other graph--

00:30:18.480 --> 00:30:20.780
the next question is, once
you've done this modeling,

00:30:20.780 --> 00:30:25.310
what is your cutoff threshold?

00:30:25.310 --> 00:30:27.770
Like, are you going
to take 50% and above

00:30:27.770 --> 00:30:31.590
and call them churners,
or 30% and above?

00:30:31.590 --> 00:30:35.060
So using the same
ROC, we're plotting

00:30:35.060 --> 00:30:38.360
the false positive rate
and the true positive rate.

00:30:38.360 --> 00:30:42.150
And then each threshold
shows what that is.

00:30:42.150 --> 00:30:45.680
So our objective is to
have a high true positive

00:30:45.680 --> 00:30:47.940
and a low false positive rate.

00:30:47.940 --> 00:30:53.210
So about at 0.3, we have
about 20% false positive

00:30:53.210 --> 00:30:54.920
and 80% is true positive.

00:30:54.920 --> 00:30:58.910
Which I feel is a good
trade-off between them,

00:30:58.910 --> 00:31:01.220
because the worst
case scenario is

00:31:01.220 --> 00:31:04.430
that you're going to send some
extra emails or messaging.

00:31:04.430 --> 00:31:08.570
Doesn't hurt if those
people didn't actually

00:31:08.570 --> 00:31:09.700
turn out to be churners.

00:31:13.430 --> 00:31:15.540
And we're plotting
out the weights.

00:31:15.540 --> 00:31:18.540
So on the left side, these
are the positive weights.

00:31:18.540 --> 00:31:21.120
And this is another
SQL from BQML.

00:31:21.120 --> 00:31:22.740
And these are the
negative weights.

00:31:22.740 --> 00:31:24.480
So the positive
weight means that they

00:31:24.480 --> 00:31:27.060
have a positive
correlation to churning,

00:31:27.060 --> 00:31:29.910
while the negative one,
which is good for us--

00:31:29.910 --> 00:31:35.790
good for retaining subscribers--
are negatively correlated.

00:31:35.790 --> 00:31:37.950
And there's two
types of features.

00:31:37.950 --> 00:31:40.370
And you have to tweak your SQL.

00:31:40.370 --> 00:31:42.120
If they're categorical
features, then you

00:31:42.120 --> 00:31:46.470
have to unnest and use a
slightly separate syntax

00:31:46.470 --> 00:31:47.190
to extract it.

00:31:47.190 --> 00:31:49.430
So we've plotted both of them.

00:31:49.430 --> 00:31:52.740
I've deliberately
erased the features

00:31:52.740 --> 00:31:55.320
because that gives out
some of the key findings

00:31:55.320 --> 00:31:56.730
that we made from this.

00:32:01.090 --> 00:32:08.310
So these are the benefits
that we see from BigQuery ML.

00:32:08.310 --> 00:32:12.030
When I heard about it-- and
I had previous experience

00:32:12.030 --> 00:32:14.550
building churn modeling--

00:32:14.550 --> 00:32:18.870
I actually built out that
SQL, a very one feature-based

00:32:18.870 --> 00:32:21.450
churn model, in a day.

00:32:21.450 --> 00:32:23.830
Since we had that
data sitting there,

00:32:23.830 --> 00:32:25.710
it was a matter of
coming up with a SQL.

00:32:25.710 --> 00:32:29.500
As you've seen in the demo,
it's not very difficult.

00:32:29.500 --> 00:32:33.850
And then we spent a few
sprints refining that model.

00:32:33.850 --> 00:32:36.450
And it really was like
getting more features,

00:32:36.450 --> 00:32:39.970
testing it out, looking at the
matrix, does it make sense?

00:32:39.970 --> 00:32:45.480
So it was that cycle was
what we did in sprint.

00:32:45.480 --> 00:32:49.650
And also, of course, built
that dashboard in Looker.

00:32:49.650 --> 00:32:51.360
If it was some
other sci-kit learn,

00:32:51.360 --> 00:32:55.170
you would have been pulling this
data out into another system.

00:32:55.170 --> 00:32:58.320
And this two sprint could
have become like six sprints

00:32:58.320 --> 00:33:01.560
or a much longer time.

00:33:01.560 --> 00:33:04.770
And we focus on the problem
rather than the data science

00:33:04.770 --> 00:33:07.410
nuances.

00:33:07.410 --> 00:33:10.780
And the feature engineering
is clearly laid out in SQL.

00:33:10.780 --> 00:33:15.000
So if a new person looks at
it, anybody familiar with SQL

00:33:15.000 --> 00:33:17.400
can easily understand
what's going on,

00:33:17.400 --> 00:33:22.020
rather than looking at Python
or R code along with SQL.

00:33:22.020 --> 00:33:24.090
And it's easy to
onboard new members.

00:33:24.090 --> 00:33:28.380
People who are familiar with SQL
who are not actually developers

00:33:28.380 --> 00:33:32.550
but work in BI or
other departments,

00:33:32.550 --> 00:33:36.600
or who are willing to learn
SQL, can actually use this.

00:33:36.600 --> 00:33:42.660
And then also, it utilizes
the scale of BigQuery.

00:33:42.660 --> 00:33:46.140
So since it's a distributed
[INAUDIBLE] based thing,

00:33:46.140 --> 00:33:51.041
you can run your models on
larger datasets in a quicker

00:33:51.041 --> 00:33:51.540
time.

00:33:57.170 --> 00:33:58.380
All right.

00:33:58.380 --> 00:34:00.216
Thank you.

